# Regress programs: one per defect, named by the defect's ledger id.
#
# tests/regress/<id>.c checks that one defect is gone. It is written as a
# suite is, with tests/test.h and no wait without a bound, and asserts the
# defect's symptom, so it fails while the defect is there and passes once it
# is fixed. As with a suite, a program that skips every test fails the run.
# Each id below is in one of two states:
#
#   gating   The defect is fixed. ninja regress runs the program through
#            tests/run.sh, as ninja test runs the suites, and fails unless
#            it passes. CI runs ninja regress in every job that runs
#            ninja test.
#   pending  The defect is not fixed yet, so the program fails for now.
#            ninja regress-pending runs it and prints the id with PASS or
#            FAIL, and does not fail on the result. It must still build:
#            while one does not, ninja regress-pending runs none of them.
#            CI runs it in the regress-pending job, which gates nothing
#            and then still lists every pending id, one that did not
#            build as FAIL.
#
# The PR that fixes a pending defect marks its id gating. A PR that fixes a
# defect with no program here adds tests/regress/<id>.c, shows that it fails
# without the fix, and lists the id as gating.
#
# configure reads this file and writes the build edges, so run ./configure
# again after editing it; the regress-pending job reads its pending ids too.
# Format: one "id state" pair per line, "#" starts a comment. An id has only
# letters, digits and dashes.

RT-main-exit            gating
RT-fchmodat-flags       gating
FMT-print-double-oob    gating
TIME-sleep-eintr        pending
